from .lg import S

PROBLEMS = [
(37, ("(JEE Advanced, integer) 2-, 3- and 4-Chlorotoluene are treated separately with excess NaNH<sub>2</sub> in liquid NH<sub>3</sub> (&minus;33 &deg;C). Let x, y and z be the numbers of <i>different</i> constitutionally isomeric toluidines (aminotoluenes) that can form from the 2-, 3- and 4-isomer respectively, assuming that every accessible benzyne is formed and each is attacked at both of its carbons. Find x + y + z.",
      S([("2-chlorotoluene", "Cc1ccccc1Cl"), ("3-chlorotoluene", "Cc1cccc(Cl)c1"), ("4-chlorotoluene", "Cc1ccc(Cl)cc1")]),
      "7",
      "2-Chlorotoluene has only one ortho hydrogen (C-3), so only 3-methylbenzyne forms; attack at either carbon gives o- and m-toluidine, x = 2. 3-Chlorotoluene has two ortho hydrogens (C-2 and C-4): the 2,3-aryne gives o- and m-toluidine, the 3,4-aryne gives m- and p-toluidine; the union is o, m, p, so y = 3. 4-Chlorotoluene gives 4-methylbenzyne only, hence m- and p-toluidine, z = 2. Sum = 2 + 3 + 2 = 7 (only three distinct toluidines exist overall, but the question counts per substrate).")),

(38, ("(Roberts experiment) Chlorobenzene labelled with <sup>14</sup>C only at C-1 (the carbon carrying Cl) is treated with KNH<sub>2</sub> in liquid NH<sub>3</sub>. The aniline obtained is degraded and the position of the label determined. (a) What distribution of <sup>14</sup>C between C-1 and C-2 of aniline is observed? (b) What distribution would an SNAr (Meisenheimer) or SN1 pathway require? (c) Why is the observed result a proof of a symmetrical intermediate?",
      S([("chlorobenzene-1-<sup>14</sup>C", "Cl[14c]1ccccc1")]),
      "about 1 : 1 (C-1 : C-2); a direct substitution would give 100 : 0",
      "Amide removes the ortho hydrogen (C-2&ndash;H) and chloride leaves from C-1, giving benzyne in which the labelled carbon and its neighbour are equivalent by symmetry. NH<sub>2</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> adds to either carbon with equal probability, and protonation of the resulting aryl anion gives aniline with the label equally at C-1 and C-2 (the <sup>14</sup>C isotope effect on the addition is negligible). SNAr or SN1 replaces Cl at the carbon that carried it, so all the label would remain at C-1. The roughly 1:1 distribution therefore requires an intermediate in which C-1 and C-2 become equivalent, the elimination&ndash;addition (aryne) mechanism.")),

(39, ("(JEE Advanced, assertion&ndash;reason) <b>Assertion:</b> 2-Chloro-1,3-dimethylbenzene is recovered unchanged when treated with NaNH<sub>2</sub> in liquid NH<sub>3</sub>, whereas chlorobenzene is converted into aniline. <b>Reason:</b> The benzyne mechanism requires a hydrogen atom ortho to the leaving group, and both ortho positions of 2-chloro-1,3-dimethylbenzene carry methyl groups. (a) Both true and Reason explains Assertion; (b) both true but Reason does not explain; (c) Assertion true, Reason false; (d) Assertion false, Reason true.",
      S([("2-chloro-1,3-dimethylbenzene", "Cc1cccc(C)c1Cl")]),
      "(a)",
      "Elimination&ndash;addition needs amide to abstract an ortho proton in an E2/E1cB-like step. Here the two ortho carbons are quaternary (CH<sub>3</sub>-bearing), so no benzyne can form. The ring has no activating nitro-type groups, so SNAr is impossible, and an aryl cation (SN1) is far too unstable. The benzylic C&ndash;H of the methyls is not a suitable site for forming an aryne. The Reason is true and is exactly why the Assertion holds.")),

(40, ("(Match the column) Match each benzyne-generating system in Column I with the small species expelled in forming the aryne, Column II. <br>Column I: (P) benzenediazonium-2-carboxylate, warm; (Q) 2-(trimethylsilyl)phenyl triflate + CsF; (R) 1-bromo-2-fluorobenzene + Mg, THF; (S) 1,2-dibromobenzene + n-BuLi, then warming from &minus;100 &deg;C; (T) bromobenzene + NaNH<sub>2</sub>/liq. NH<sub>3</sub>. <br>Column II: (1) N<sub>2</sub> + CO<sub>2</sub>; (2) Me<sub>3</sub>SiF + triflate ion; (3) MgBrF; (4) n-BuBr + LiBr; (5) NH<sub>3</sub> + NaBr.",
      None,
      "P&ndash;1, Q&ndash;2, R&ndash;3, S&ndash;4, T&ndash;5",
      "P: the zwitterion loses N<sub>2</sub> and CO<sub>2</sub> (explosive when dry). Q: fluoride attacks silicon, the C&ndash;Si bond becomes a carbanion that expels triflate (Kobayashi), giving Me<sub>3</sub>SiF and TfO<sup>&minus;</sup>. R: the Grignard forms at the weaker C&ndash;Br bond and the o-fluoro arylmagnesium bromide eliminates MgBrF. S: Br/Li exchange gives n-BuBr and 2-bromophenyllithium, which loses LiBr on warming. T: amide removes the ortho proton and bromide leaves, giving NH<sub>3</sub> and NaBr.")),

(41, ("Benzenediazonium-2-carboxylate (from anthranilic acid and isoamyl nitrite) is decomposed in refluxing 1,2-dichloroethane in the presence of excess furan to give <b>A</b> (C<sub>10</sub>H<sub>8</sub>O). Treatment of <b>A</b> with dilute HCl gives an isomer <b>B</b> that dissolves in aqueous NaOH and gives a violet colour with FeCl<sub>3</sub>. Identify A and B and state the number of stereoisomers of A.",
      S([("A", "C1=CC2OC1c1ccccc12"), ("B", "Oc1cccc2ccccc12")]),
      "A = 1,4-dihydro-1,4-epoxynaphthalene (achiral, 1 stereoisomer); B = 1-naphthol",
      "Benzyne acts as a dienophile towards furan in a [4+2] cycloaddition, giving the oxa-bridged bicycle A (7-oxabenzonorbornadiene). Its mirror plane through O and the midpoint of the C=C makes it achiral (one stereoisomer). Acid protonates the bridging oxygen; C&ndash;O cleavage gives a stabilised benzylic/allylic cation (C-1) with OH at C-4; a 1,2-hydride shift from C-4 (or loss of H<sup>+</sup> from the corresponding ketonic species) and tautomerisation gives the aromatic phenol, 1-naphthol (FeCl<sub>3</sub> test).")),

(42, ("(Match the column) Match each reaction with the mechanism by which the substitution occurs. Reactions: (P) 2,4-dinitrochlorobenzene + piperidine; (Q) chlorobenzene + KNH<sub>2</sub>/liq. NH<sub>3</sub>; (R) benzenediazonium tetrafluoroborate in warm water giving phenol; (S) iodobenzene + K<sup>+</sup> &minus;CH<sub>2</sub>COCH<sub>3</sub>, liq. NH<sub>3</sub>, hv, giving phenylacetone; (T) 4-chloronitrobenzene + NaOMe. Mechanisms: (i) SNAr, addition&ndash;elimination via a Meisenheimer complex; (ii) elimination&ndash;addition via benzyne; (iii) SN1 (aryl cation); (iv) S<sub>RN</sub>1 (radical chain with radical-anion intermediates).",
      None,
      "P&ndash;i, Q&ndash;ii, R&ndash;iii, S&ndash;iv, T&ndash;i",
      "P and T have o/p nitro groups that stabilise the anionic sigma-complex (SNAr). Q has no activating group and a strong base: ortho-deprotonation/loss of Cl<sup>&minus;</sup> gives benzyne. R: ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup> loses N<sub>2</sub>, an excellent leaving group, giving Ar<sup>+</sup> captured by water. S: photostimulated electron transfer to PhI gives PhI<sup>&bull;&minus;</sup>, which fragments to Ph<sup>&bull;</sup> + I<sup>&minus;</sup>; Ph<sup>&bull;</sup> adds to the enolate and the product radical anion transfers an electron to a new PhI (chain).")),

(43, ("2-Bromoanisole and 3-bromoanisole are treated separately with NaNH<sub>2</sub> in liquid NH<sub>3</sub>. (a) Show that both give the <i>same single</i> methoxyaniline and name it. (b) Classify the substitution as ipso or cine for each substrate. (c) Explain the regiochemistry of amide addition to the aryne.",
      S([("2-bromoanisole", "COc1ccccc1Br"), ("3-bromoanisole", "COc1cccc(Br)c1"), ("product", "COc1cccc(N)c1")]),
      "both give 3-methoxyaniline; 2-bromoanisole: cine, 3-bromoanisole: ipso",
      "2-Bromoanisole can lose only the C-3 proton, and 3-bromoanisole loses the doubly activated C-2 proton (between OMe and Br, both &minus;I); both give 3-methoxybenzyne. OMe is &minus;I, so amide adds to the aryne carbon <i>meta</i> to OMe (C-3 in the numbering of the anisole), leaving the carbanion on the carbon adjacent to the OMe-bearing carbon where the inductive effect stabilises it. In 2-bromoanisole this carbon is the one that bore H (NH<sub>2</sub> enters next to the old C&ndash;Br position: cine); in 3-bromoanisole it is the carbon that bore Br (ipso). ⚑ Selectivity is very high but not absolute: the minor C-4 deprotonation of 3-bromoanisole would give 4-methoxybenzyne, which is expected to give a meta/para mixture.")),

(44, ("Benzyne generated from benzenediazonium-2-carboxylate in the presence of anthracene gives the hydrocarbon <b>T</b>. (a) Give the molecular formula and name of T. (b) How many signals are expected in its <sup>1</sup>H NMR and <sup>13</sup>C NMR spectra, and what is the <sup>1</sup>H integration ratio? (Assume ideal D<sub>3h</sub> symmetry.)",
      S([("T", "C12c3ccccc3C(c3ccccc13)c1ccccc12")]),
      "T = triptycene, C<sub>20</sub>H<sub>14</sub>; 3 <sup>1</sup>H signals (2H : 6H : 6H) and 4 <sup>13</sup>C signals",
      "Benzyne adds across the 9,10-positions of anthracene ([4+2], the central ring acting as the diene), giving triptycene (9,10-dihydro-9,10-benzenoanthracene), C<sub>14</sub>H<sub>10</sub> + C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub> = C<sub>20</sub>H<sub>14</sub>. In the D<sub>3h</sub> structure the two bridgehead CH are equivalent (2H singlet, about 5.4 ppm); the 18 aromatic H fall into two sets of six (positions 1,4 type and 2,3 type, an AA&prime;BB&prime; pattern). In the <sup>13</sup>C spectrum: bridgehead CH, quaternary ring-fusion C (all six equivalent), and two types of aromatic CH, so 4 signals.")),

(45, ("Benzyne (from 2-(trimethylsilyl)phenyl triflate/CsF) is allowed to react separately with (i) tetraphenylcyclopentadienone and (ii) 2-pyrone. In each case a bicyclic [4+2] adduct forms but cannot be isolated; a gas is lost spontaneously and an aromatic hydrocarbon results. Identify each hydrocarbon (with molecular formula) and the gas lost.",
      S([("tetracyclone", "O=C1C(c2ccccc2)=C(c2ccccc2)C(c2ccccc2)=C1c1ccccc1"), ("2-pyrone", "O=c1cccco1")]),
      "(i) 1,2,3,4-tetraphenylnaphthalene, C<sub>34</sub>H<sub>24</sub> + CO; (ii) naphthalene, C<sub>10</sub>H<sub>8</sub> + CO<sub>2</sub>",
      "(i) The cyclopentadienone is a 4&pi; diene; the adduct is a 7-norbornadienone, which extrudes CO in a cheletropic retro-[4+2] to aromatise: C<sub>29</sub>H<sub>20</sub>O + C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub> &minus; CO = C<sub>34</sub>H<sub>24</sub>. (ii) 2-Pyrone reacts across C-3/C-6 to give a 2-oxabicyclo[2.2.2]octadienone (bridged lactone); retro-Diels&ndash;Alder expels CO<sub>2</sub> and leaves the new benzene ring of naphthalene: C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>4</sub>O<sub>2</sub> + C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub> &minus; CO<sub>2</sub> = C<sub>10</sub>H<sub>8</sub>.")),

(46, ("Benzyne is generated (CsF, 2-(trimethylsilyl)phenyl triflate) in neat isobutene (2-methylpropene). The main product <b>P</b> (C<sub>10</sub>H<sub>12</sub>) arises by a concerted pericyclic reaction in which aryne acts as the enophile. (a) Name the reaction and give P. (b) Name one minor isomeric product. (c) If (CD<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>C=CH<sub>2</sub> were used, how many deuterium atoms would sit on the benzene ring of P?",
      S([("P", "C=C(C)Cc1ccccc1")]),
      "ene reaction; P = (2-methylallyl)benzene; minor: 1,1-dimethylbenzocyclobutene ([2+2]); 1 D on the ring",
      "Isobutene has allylic hydrogens, so benzyne undergoes an ene reaction: the CH<sub>2</sub>= terminus bonds to one aryne carbon, an allylic H from a CH<sub>3</sub> group is delivered to the neighbouring aryne carbon, and the double bond shifts to give PhCH<sub>2</sub>C(CH<sub>3</sub>)=CH<sub>2</sub>, C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub> + C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>8</sub> = C<sub>10</sub>H<sub>12</sub>. The competing stepwise or concerted [2+2] gives 1,1-dimethylbenzocyclobutene. ⚑ The ene:[2+2] ratio depends on the aryne source and conditions. With the CD<sub>3</sub> compound the transferred atom is D, so the ring carries 1 D (ortho to the new CH<sub>2</sub>), the chain CD<sub>3</sub> and =CD<sub>2</sub> carry the other five.")),

(47, ("Benzyne (Kobayashi method) is generated in the presence of (i) benzyl azide and (ii) the nitrone PhCH=N<sup>+</sup>(CH<sub>3</sub>)O<sup>&minus;</sup>. Both react as 1,3-dipoles. Give the products and the total number of ring heteroatoms in the new heterocycle in each case.",
      S([("(i) product", "c1ccc2c(c1)nnn2Cc1ccccc1"), ("(ii) product", "CN1Oc2ccccc2C1c1ccccc1")]),
      "(i) 1-benzylbenzotriazole (3 ring N); (ii) 2-methyl-3-phenyl-2,3-dihydro-1,2-benzisoxazole (ring N and O, 2 heteroatoms)",
      "A [3+2] dipolar cycloaddition of the 4&pi; dipole across the aryne &pi; bond (the in-plane, strained one) gives a five-membered ring fused to the benzene ring. Benzyl azide adds with its terminal N (N<sub>3</sub>) and substituted N (N<sub>1</sub>) to the two aryne carbons: aromatic 1-benzyl-1H-benzotriazole, which has three nitrogens in the ring. The nitrone adds with O bonded to one aryne carbon and the nitrone carbon to the other, giving the benzo-fused isoxazolidine (benzisoxazoline): N and O in the ring. Because benzyne itself is symmetrical, no regioisomers arise from the aryne side.")),

(48, ("(Multi-correct) Which of the following statements about nucleophilic addition to 3-substituted and 4-substituted benzynes are correct? (A) Amines add to 3-methoxybenzyne mainly at the aryne carbon meta to OMe (C-1). (B) In the distortion model this is accounted for by the aryne carbon meta to OMe having the larger internal angle (being flatter), so the transition state for attack there needs less distortion. (C) Attack at C-1 places the developing negative charge on C-2, next to the oxygen-bearing carbon, where the inductive (&minus;I) effect of OMe stabilises it. (D) 4-Methoxybenzyne is expected to be attacked with high regioselectivity because OMe is conjugated with the triple bond. (E) 3-Methylbenzyne, with a +I substituent, shows an even stronger regiochemical preference than 3-methoxybenzyne.",
      None,
      "A, B and C",
      "A, B and C are the standard rationale (inductive rule/Garg&ndash;Houk distortion model): a &minus;I 3-substituent directs the nucleophile meta to itself; the aryne is bent more strongly at the carbon nearer the substituent, so the distal carbon is the flatter, more electrophilic site. D is false: in a 4-substituted aryne the substituent is remote and the two carbons are almost equally accessible, so meta/para mixtures are obtained. E is false: methyl is a weak perturbation, so selectivity in 3-methylbenzyne is low. ⚑ The distortion angles are computed values.")),

(49, ("(Integer) 2-Chloropyridine and 3-bromopyridine are treated separately with NaNH<sub>2</sub> in liquid NH<sub>3</sub>. Let n be the total number of different aminopyridines isolated from the two reactions (counting each isomer once per reaction). Which of the two reactions involves an aryne? What is n?",
      S([("2-chloropyridine", "Clc1ccccn1"), ("3-bromopyridine", "Brc1cccnc1")]),
      "3-bromopyridine (3,4-pyridyne); n = 3",
      "In 2-chloropyridine the C-2 carbon is activated by the ring nitrogen (like an ortho-nitro position), so NH<sub>2</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> displaces Cl by addition&ndash;elimination (Meisenheimer-type anion with charge on N): only 2-aminopyridine (1 product). 3-Bromopyridine has no such activation at C-3; amide removes H-4 and bromide leaves, giving 3,4-pyridyne, which is attacked at C-3 or C-4 to give 3- and 4-aminopyridine (2 products). Total n = 1 + 2 = 3. ⚑ By the same logic, 4-chloropyridine is intercepted mainly by SNAr, and the proportion of 3,4-pyridyne there depends on conditions.")),

(50, ("N-Methyl-6,7-indolyne (a 1-methylindole with the triple bond between C-6 and C-7) is generated in the presence of an amine R<sub>2</sub>NH (e.g. morpholine). Two constitutional isomers can form. Predict the major one and justify the regiochemistry from the inductive rule.",
      S([("6-amino isomer", "Cn1ccc2ccc(N3CCOCC3)cc21"), ("7-amino isomer", "Cn1ccc2cccc(N3CCOCC3)c21")]),
      "6-(dialkylamino)-1-methylindole (attack at C-6)",
      "C-7 is adjacent to the ring-fusion carbon C-7a, which bears the electronegative nitrogen. Seen as a 3-substituted benzyne, the heteroatom-bearing carbon exerts a &minus;I effect, so the nucleophile adds at the carbon meta to it (C-6) and the aryl anion develops at C-7, next to C-7a, where it is inductively stabilised. The distortion model agrees: C-6 is the flatter carbon. Hence the 6-amino indole predominates. ⚑ Selectivity is high but not complete, and it is much lower for the 5,6-indolyne, which has no such bias.")),

(51, ("(Multi-correct) Which statements about strained alkynes and cyclic allenes are correct? (A) Cyclooctyne is the smallest cycloalkyne that can be isolated as a stable compound at room temperature. (B) Cyclohexyne and cycloheptyne are known only as transient intermediates, detected by trapping (for example with furan or dienes). (C) Cyclooctynes react with organic azides at room temperature without a catalyst largely because strain is released in the [3+2] cycloaddition. (D) 1,2-Cyclohexadiene is a strongly bent, chiral allene that can be trapped as a transient intermediate. (E) The C&ndash;C&equiv;C angle in cyclooctyne is nearly 180&deg;.",
      None,
      "A, B, C and D",
      "A: cyclooctyne is isolable (it polymerises slowly), smaller rings are not. B: cyclohexyne (e.g. from 2-(trimethylsilyl)cyclohex-1-enyl triflate/fluoride) and cycloheptyne are intercepted in situ. C: the bent alkyne carries roughly 18 kcal/mol of strain, which accounts for strain-promoted azide&ndash;alkyne cycloadditions (Bertozzi-type click chemistry). D: 1,2-cyclohexadiene has a C=C=C angle far from 180&deg; (about 130&deg;), is chiral because the allene is twisted, and is trapped by furan or styrene. E is false: the angle is about 158&deg;, which is the source of strain. ⚑ Numerical values are computational or crystallographic estimates.")),

(52, ("(Bergman cyclisation) Cyclodeca-3-ene-1,5-diyne (a ten-membered ring with a <i>cis</i> C=C between two C&equiv;C) undergoes thermal cycloaromatisation at about 37 &deg;C (t<sub>1/2</sub> a matter of hours). In the presence of 1,4-cyclohexadiene, (a) which diradical forms? (b) what is the final organic product from the enediyne (give formula) and what does 1,4-cyclohexadiene become? (c) why is the ring size important?",
      S([("enediyne", "C1CCCC#CC=CC#C1"), ("product", "C1CCc2ccccc2C1")]),
      "p-benzyne (5,6,7,8-tetrahydronaphthalene-1,4-diyl); tetralin C<sub>10</sub>H<sub>12</sub> and benzene",
      "The Bergman reaction forms a C&ndash;C bond between the two inner alkyne carbons (C-1 and C-6), creating a benzene ring bearing two radical centres para to each other (a 1,4-didehydroarene, p-benzyne). The remaining four CH<sub>2</sub> groups form the saturated ring. The singlet diradical abstracts two H atoms from 1,4-cyclohexadiene (which becomes benzene), giving tetralin, C<sub>10</sub>H<sub>10</sub> + 2H = C<sub>10</sub>H<sub>12</sub>. Ring size matters because it fixes the distance between the reacting alkyne termini: in cyclic enediynes a distance of about 3.2&ndash;3.3 &Aring; is roughly the threshold between kinetically stable and spontaneously cyclising compounds (shorter cyclises faster) (Nicolaou criterion).")),

(53, ("(IChO-style counting) The following chloroxylenes are heated separately with NaNH<sub>2</sub> in liquid NH<sub>3</sub>: <b>A</b> 4-chloro-1,2-dimethylbenzene, <b>B</b> 5-chloro-1,3-dimethylbenzene, <b>C</b> 2-chloro-1,3-dimethylbenzene, <b>D</b> 1-chloro-2,3-dimethylbenzene. Assume every possible benzyne forms and is attacked at both ends (no regiochemical bias). How many <i>different</i> dimethylanilines are obtained altogether from A + B + C + D? Name them.",
      S([("A", "Cc1ccc(Cl)cc1C"), ("B", "Cc1cc(C)cc(Cl)c1"), ("C", "Cc1cccc(C)c1Cl"), ("D", "Cc1cccc(Cl)c1C")]),
      "4: 2,3-, 3,4-, 2,4- and 3,5-dimethylaniline",
      "A: two ortho H (C-3, C-5). The 3,4-aryne gives 3,4- and 2,3-dimethylaniline; the 4,5-aryne gives 3,4-dimethylaniline from either end. B: both ortho H are equivalent; the single aryne gives 3,5-dimethylaniline (NH<sub>2</sub> at the old C&ndash;Cl carbon) and 2,4-dimethylaniline (NH<sub>2</sub> at the other end, now flanked by one Me ortho and one para). C: both ortho carbons carry Me, so no aryne forms and no amine results. D: only H-6 is available; the aryne gives 2,3-dimethylaniline (ipso) and 3,4-dimethylaniline. Distinct set = {2,3; 3,4; 2,4; 3,5} = 4 (six products are listed if counted per substrate, but 2,3- and 3,4- each appear twice).")),

(54, ("Benzyne generated without a trap dimerises to biphenylene and trimerises to triphenylene. (a) Give the formulas of these two hydrocarbons. (b) 4-Methylbenzyne is generated under the same conditions. How many different dimethylbiphenylenes form on dimerisation, and which are they? (Biphenylene positions 1&ndash;4 and 5&ndash;8; the ring-fusion carbons are 4a, 4b, 8a, 8b.)",
      S([("biphenylene", "c1ccc2c(c1)-c1ccccc1-2"), ("triphenylene", "c1ccc2c(c1)c1ccccc1c1ccccc21")]),
      "(a) C<sub>12</sub>H<sub>8</sub> and C<sub>18</sub>H<sub>12</sub>; (b) two: 2,6- and 2,7-dimethylbiphenylene",
      "Dimerisation is a formal [2+2] of two arynes (biphenylene, C<sub>12</sub>H<sub>8</sub>); a formal [2+2+2] of three gives triphenylene (C<sub>18</sub>H<sub>12</sub>). In 4-methylbenzyne the Me is para to one aryne carbon (C<sub>p</sub>) and meta to the other (C<sub>m</sub>). Two molecules can join with C<sub>p</sub>&ndash;C<sub>p</sub> and C<sub>m</sub>&ndash;C<sub>m</sub> bonds, giving the 2,7-isomer (C<sub>2v</sub>), or with C<sub>p</sub>&ndash;C<sub>m</sub> bonds, giving the 2,6-isomer (C<sub>2h</sub>). No other pairing exists, so two products form (1:1 within statistics, ignoring regiochemical bias).")),

(55, ("Ethyl acetoacetate, 2-(trimethylsilyl)phenyl triflate and CsF in MeCN give mainly an <i>acyl&ndash;alkylation</i> product <b>X</b> (C<sub>12</sub>H<sub>14</sub>O<sub>3</sub>) rather than the simple C-arylation product (ethyl 2-phenylacetoacetate). Identify X and describe the key steps of its formation.",
      S([("X", "CCOC(=O)Cc1ccccc1C(C)=O"), ("simple C-arylation", "CCOC(=O)C(C(C)=O)c1ccccc1")]),
      "X = ethyl 2-(2-acetylphenyl)acetate (C&ndash;C &sigma;-bond insertion)",
      "Fluoride generates benzyne and also the enolate of the 1,3-dicarbonyl. Step 1: the enolate carbon adds to benzyne giving an aryl anion. Step 2: this anion attacks the neighbouring ketone C=O intramolecularly, forming a strained benzocyclobutenoxide. Step 3: ring-opening by cleavage of the former acyl C&ndash;C bond is driven by relief of strain and formation of a stabilised ester enolate; protonation gives the ortho-acylated arylacetate. The net result is insertion of the aryne into the C&ndash;C &sigma; bond between the carbonyl and the &alpha;-carbon. ⚑ The ratio of insertion to ordinary arylation depends on solvent and counter-ion.")),

(56, ("(Multi-step synthesis) 3-Phenylphthalide is treated with PhMgBr (1 equiv) then aqueous work-up to give a lactol <b>L</b>; acid dehydrates L to the intensely fluorescent <b>D</b> (C<sub>20</sub>H<sub>14</sub>O). D is allowed to react with benzyne to give <b>E</b> (C<sub>26</sub>H<sub>18</sub>O), and E is deoxygenated (low-valent titanium) to <b>F</b> (C<sub>26</sub>H<sub>18</sub>). Identify D, E and F.",
      S([("3-phenylphthalide", "O=C1OC(c2ccccc2)c2ccccc12"), ("D", "c1ccc(cc1)-c1oc(-c2ccccc2)c2ccccc12"), ("E", "c1ccc(cc1)C23OC(c1ccccc1)(c1ccccc12)c1ccccc13")]),
      "D = 1,3-diphenylisobenzofuran; E = its benzyne [4+2] adduct (9,10-diphenyl-9,10-epoxy-9,10-dihydroanthracene); F = 9,10-diphenylanthracene",
      "PhMgBr adds to the lactone carbonyl to give the hemiketal (lactol) L, which on acid loses water to give the o-quinoid isobenzofuran D, a very reactive diene (1,3-diphenylisobenzofuran, C<sub>20</sub>H<sub>14</sub>O). Benzyne adds across the 1,3-positions in a Diels&ndash;Alder reaction giving the oxa-bridged adduct E (C<sub>20</sub>H<sub>14</sub>O + C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub> = C<sub>26</sub>H<sub>18</sub>O). Reductive removal of the bridging oxygen then aromatises the central ring to 9,10-diphenylanthracene F, C<sub>26</sub>H<sub>18</sub>.")),

(57, ("(Olympiad-style mechanism) The enediyne antitumour antibiotic calicheamicin &gamma;<sub>1</sub><sup>I</sup> contains a methyl trisulfide trigger. Arrange the events in order and name the key intermediate: (1) abstraction of H atoms from deoxyribose of DNA; (2) intramolecular Michael addition of the new thiolate to the enone of the bicyclic core; (3) Bergman cyclisation; (4) reduction of the trisulfide by cellular thiol; (5) change of hybridisation/geometry that brings the alkyne termini closer. Also state how the Myers&ndash;Saito reaction of neocarzinostatin chromophore differs in its diradical.",
      None,
      "4, 2, 5, 3, 1; key intermediate p-benzyne; Myers&ndash;Saito gives an &alpha;,3-didehydrotoluene (&sigma;,&pi;) biradical",
      "A thiol cleaves the S&ndash;S bond of the trisulfide (4). The resulting thiolate adds in a conjugate fashion to the enone (2), converting an sp<sup>2</sup> carbon to sp<sup>3</sup> and thereby bending the bicycle so that the two alkyne termini approach (5). Bergman cyclisation (3) then gives the 1,4-didehydrobenzene (p-benzyne) diradical, which abstracts H atoms from deoxyribose of the two DNA strands (typically C-5&prime; on one strand and C-4&prime; or C-5&prime; on the other) (1) causing double-strand cleavage. Neocarzinostatin's activated enyne&ndash;allene cyclises by the Myers&ndash;Saito reaction (C2&ndash;C7 bond) to an &alpha;,3-didehydrotoluene biradical with one &sigma; and one benzylic &pi; radical centre.")),

(58, ("(Olympiad-style) The rate of reaction of halobenzenes PhX with KNH<sub>2</sub> in liquid NH<sub>3</sub> (aryne pathway) follows Br &gt; I &gt; Cl &gt;&gt; F, whereas in SNAr reactions of 2,4-dinitrohalobenzenes with piperidine the order is F &gt;&gt; Cl &asymp; Br &gt; I. (a) Explain why the orders differ. (b) In KND<sub>2</sub>/ND<sub>3</sub>, fluorobenzene exchanges its ortho hydrogens for deuterium much faster than it forms aniline. What does this say about the mechanism?",
      None,
      "aryne: C&ndash;X cleavage is rate-limiting-type; SNAr: addition is rate-limiting; fluorobenzene: fast reversible deprotonation, slow loss of F<sup>&minus;</sup>",
      "In SNAr the slow step is nucleophilic attack to give the Meisenheimer complex; fluorine, the most electronegative halogen, makes the ipso carbon most electrophilic and stabilises the anionic sigma-complex, so F is fastest and C&ndash;X cleavage (fast step) is not rate-limiting. In the aryne route, ortho-deprotonation gives an aryl anion and then expulsion of X<sup>&minus;</sup> occurs with significant C&ndash;X bond breaking in the rate-limiting stage, so the better leaving groups (Br, I) win and the strong C&ndash;F bond is slowest. Hence fluorobenzene is deprotonated easily (strong &minus;I of F acidifies the ortho H) but the o-fluorophenyl anion mostly returns to substrate, which gives H/D exchange without much aniline: the elimination is a two-step E1cB-like process. ⚑ The exact order of Br and I depends on conditions.")),
]
